\f1\fs22 The cheeseburger helped for about ten minutes. Then Chanta sank into a fugue that lasted a week.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He missed Sarah, obviously. He\'92d released her through song, exhorted the Elvi to do likewise\'97and thank tax he had. Because without that release, the Elvi would have torn apart the Ghetto bathroom sink by bathroom sink, until there was nothing left but rubble and porcelain dust. Ulyssia was one bad lyric away from an Elvi uprising.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta had bigger problems than musical civil war. He missed Sarah, sure. He missed Little Clinton more. There was something about the little fella that calmed and soothed him. Little Clinton was his muse, Chanta suspected, dictating more by psychic woo woo than mer inspiration the lyrics that earned Chanta the title Great Teacher.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Of course, that was all nonsense, Chanta told himself. But in the days that followed, the absence of Sarah and bub deepened his depression. He refused to leave the ruins of the mansion, as if by staying in the wreckage he was nearer to them. He refused to eat, aside from the odd cheeseburger. He couldn't sleep, partly because he spent nights under a dusty blanket, sitting knees-up, wedged into the one remaining solid corner of what was once his bedroom.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On the seventh day, the first High Elvis of the newly formed Elvi Parliament of the Ghetto came for him. The High Elvis took Chanta's elbow gently, coaxed him reverently to his feet, and led him to a parliamentary Cadillac. Chanta lay down on the back seat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Cadillac, driven by the High Elvis himself, carried Chanta through the Ghetto to new lodgings. Had Chanta been present enough to look out the window, he might have recognised the open market where, on the night of the Vegas Show, Kleo bought his jumpsuit of many colours, Solo his leathers, and Chilo his army uniform. But Chanta stared at the ceiling upholstery and saw nothing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Cadillac pulled up less than two blocks from Graceland. Three months ago\'97well, a little less\'97Chanta attended his first-ever Vegas Show there. Two Elvi emerged from a ramshackle building, typical Ghetto architecture\'97dusty brickwork and gaunt windows sagging forward so severely that a hearty child's breath might send the whole structure tumbling into the street. They eased Chanta from the back seat. He took a moment to find balance and strength in his legs, then looked up at the fa\'e7ade leaning ominously over the footpath. The Elvi assisted him through the doors.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Expensive department store air conditioning hit them like a frozen towel to the face.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Belying the third-world exterior, the interior had been recently gutted and retrofitted as a very modern, very luxurious recording studio. There were control rooms, a large studio that could fit an orchestra, a couple of smaller studios, vocal rooms for every mood, and a drum room. Technically, there was a drum room for every kind of drummer, which\'97drummers being what they are\'97meant there was precisely one drum room. Drummers, for their part, suspected the whole place had been designed by an attention-seeking frontman. Or possibly a lead guitarist. Or, god forbid, a saxophone player. Drummers have opinions about these things, though rarely the authority to enforce them. In the back was an equally opulent two-bedroom flat, complete with full kitchen, dining, living, and media rooms, a fully stocked bar in its own room, and full-sized bathrooms off both upstairs bedrooms. The Elvi took Chanta to one of these bedrooms and deposited him on the bed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi had plans for the Chanta, the Great Teacher. They had great-ish expectations, reasonably based on past performance. They needed the Great Teacher to keep writing. There were still many great tunes out there begging for lyrics that the Elvi could stop cracking skulls over.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There was a problem. Chanta harboured a dark secret. Since the emotional outpouring of \'93Let Your Momma Go\'94, the lyrics he had written during the ensuing days were somewhat finite in number, which is to say, they numbered exactly zero.
